    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# Q4:Use the Central limit theorem to compute the interval. Change the sample size to observe the distribution of the mean of the expenses by female and male customers.\n",
        "The interval that you calculated is called Confidence Interval. The width of the interval is mostly decided by the business: Typically 90%, 95%, or 99%. Play around with the width parameter and report the observations. **bold text**"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "s-FI9J29NCgA"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "4.1 : Use the Central limit theorem to compute the interval. Change the sample size to observe the distribution of the mean of the expenses by female and male customers"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "lnzZ-F2uNShz"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "\n",
        "# Separate male and female purchase data\n",
        "male = df[df['Gender'] == 'M']['Purchase']\n",
        "female = df[df['Gender'] == 'F']['Purchase']\n",
        "\n",
        "# Function to calculate confidence interval\n",
        "def ci(data, confidence=0.95):\n",
        "    mean = np.mean(data)\n",
        "    std = np.std(data, ddof=1)\n",
        "    n = len(data)\n",
        "    z = 1.96  # for 95% CI\n",
        "    moe = z * (std / np.sqrt(n))\n",
        "    return (mean - moe, mean + moe)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Test for different sample sizes\n",
        "for size in [300, 3000, 30000]:\n",
        "    male_sample = np.random.choice(male, size=size, replace=True)\n",
        "    female_sample = np.random.choice(female, size=size, replace=True)\n",
        "\n",
        "    male_ci = ci(male_sample)\n",
        "    female_ci = ci(female_sample)\n",
        "\n",
        "    print(f\"Sample size = {size}\")\n",
        "    print(f\"Male 95% CI: {male_ci}\")\n",
        "    print(f\"Female 95% CI: {female_ci}\")\n",
        "    print(\"-\"*50)\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/",
          "height": 0
        },
        "id": "GZsWj7ZfNQJM",
        "outputId": "aec691b1-f24f-42db-8b8f-f1da5f831bad"
      },
      "execution_count": 35,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Sample size = 300\n",
            "Male 95% CI: (np.float64(8969.590067560259), np.float64(10181.836599106407))\n",
            "Female 95% CI: (np.float64(8473.098872944169), np.float64(9586.301127055833))\n",
            "--------------------------------------------------\n",
            "Sample size = 3000\n",
            "Male 95% CI: (np.float64(9331.298724208757), np.float64(9695.531275791245))\n",
            "Female 95% CI: (np.float64(8464.17191779067), np.float64(8799.843415542662))\n",
            "--------------------------------------------------\n",
            "Sample size = 30000\n",
            "Male 95% CI: (np.float64(9361.270404734225), np.float64(9476.765128599107))\n",
            "Female 95% CI: (np.float64(8688.06026550298), np.float64(8796.262467830355))\n",
            "--------------------------------------------------\n"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "As the sample size increases, the confidence intervals become narrower, meaning our estimates of average spending are getting more precise.\n",
        "The male intervals are always higher, proving that male customers spend more on average than female customers.\n",
        "This perfectly shows the Central Limit Theorem — with large samples, the sample mean becomes stable and close to the true population mean."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "FKzIBTVoN0WZ"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# 4.2 The interval that you calculated is called Confidence Interval. The width of the interval is mostly decided by the business: Typically 90%, 95%, or 99%. Play"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "S5VKzVSDN1Pr"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "\n",
        "# Function to calculate CI for any confidence level\n",
        "def ci(data, confidence):\n",
        "    mean = np.mean(data)\n",
        "    std = np.std(data, ddof=1)\n",
        "    n = len(data)\n",
        "\n",
        "    # Z-scores for different confidence levels\n",
        "    z_values = {90: 1.645, 95: 1.96, 99: 2.576}\n",
        "    z = z_values[confidence]\n",
        "\n",
        "    moe = z * (std / np.sqrt(n))\n",
        "    return (mean - moe, mean + moe)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Use large sample size for accuracy\n",
        "male_sample = np.random.choice(df[df['Gender'] == 'M']['Purchase'], size=30000, replace=True)\n",
        "female_sample = np.random.choice(df[df['Gender'] == 'F']['Purchase'], size=30000, replace=True)\n",
        "\n",
        "for conf in [90, 95, 99]:\n",
        "    male_ci = ci(male_sample, conf)\n",
        "    female_ci = ci(female_sample, conf)\n",
        "\n",
        "    print(f\"{conf}% CI for Male: {male_ci}\")\n",
        "    print(f\"{conf}% CI for Female: {female_ci}\")\n",
        "    print(\"-\"*50)\n"
      ],
