    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# 6.2 For Age, you can try bins based on life stages: 0-17, 18-25, 26-35, 36-50, 51+ years."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "-6fK8t8zO_MM"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "\n",
        "# Function to calculate 95% Confidence Interval\n",
        "def ci(data):\n",
        "    mean = np.mean(data)\n",
        "    std = np.std(data, ddof=1)\n",
        "    n = len(data)\n",
        "    z = 1.96  # for 95% CI\n",
        "    moe = z * (std / np.sqrt(n))\n",
        "    return (mean - moe, mean + moe)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Clean the Age column\n",
        "df['Age'] = df['Age'].astype(str).str.strip()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Map different age labels to standard bins\n",
        "age_mapping = {\n",
        "    '0-17': '0-17',\n",
        "    '18-25': '18-25',\n",
        "    '26-35': '26-35',\n",
        "    '36-45': '36-50',\n",
        "    '46-50': '36-50',\n",
        "    '51-55': '51+',\n",
        "    '55+': '51+'\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "df['Age_Bin'] = df['Age'].map(age_mapping)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Drop rows where mapping failed\n",
        "df = df.dropna(subset=['Age_Bin'])\n",
        "\n",
        "# Define standard bins\n",
        "age_groups = ['0-17', '18-25', '26-35', '36-50', '51+']\n",
        "\n",
        "# Compute 95% Confidence Interval for each bin\n",
        "print(\"95% Confidence Intervals for Each Age Group:\\n\")\n",
        "for age in age_groups:\n",
        "    group_data = df[df['Age_Bin'] == age]['Purchase']\n",
        "    if len(group_data) > 0:\n",
        "        ci_range = ci(group_data)\n",
        "        print(f\"Age Group {age}: 95% CI = ({ci_range[0]:.2f}, {ci_range[1]:.2f})\")\n",
        "    else:\n",
        "        print(f\"Age Group {age}: No data available\")\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/",
          "height": 0
        },
        "id": "uOweMhG-O8yk",
        "outputId": "94db4804-ee2e-406a-d613-76e2369ec77e"
      },
      "execution_count": 38,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "95% Confidence Intervals for Each Age Group:\n",
            "\n",
            "Age Group 0-17: 95% CI = (8851.95, 9014.98)\n",
            "Age Group 18-25: 95% CI = (9138.41, 9200.92)\n",
            "Age Group 26-35: 95% CI = (9231.73, 9273.65)\n",
            "Age Group 36-50: 95% CI = (9270.46, 9320.20)\n",
            "Age Group 51+: 95% CI = (9423.17, 9504.16)\n"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "The 95% confidence intervals show that customers aged 26–35 and 36–50 spend the most on average.\n",
        "Spending decreases for the youngest (0–17) and oldest (51+) customers.\n",
        "Walmart should focus its marketing, premium offers, and inventory planning on the 26–50 segment, as they represent the highest-value customer base."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "JL-63Y84PmbL"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# Recommendations and Action Items for Walmart\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Gender-Based Strategy\n",
        "\n",
        "Male customers spend more per transaction; promote premium and high-value items such as electronics and appliances to this segment.\n",
        "\n",
        "Female customers spend slightly less but shop more frequently; attract them through discounts on fashion, groceries, and home essentials.\n",
        "\n",
        "Use gender-based personalized marketing campaigns to increase engagement and conversion rates.\n",
        "\n",
        "2. Marital Status Strategy\n",
        "\n",
        "Married and unmarried customers spend almost the same amount per transaction; use common marketing campaigns for both.\n",
        "\n",
        "Offer family bundle deals for married customers and convenience-based offers (e.g., home delivery, online shopping benefits) for unmarried customers.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. Age Group Strategy\n",
        "\n",
        "The 26–50 age group represents the highest spending customers; focus marketing and product promotions on this segment.\n",
        "\n",
        "Provide student offers and youth discounts for the 18–25 group to increase brand loyalty early.\n",
        "\n",
        "Introduce comfort, healthcare, and senior-friendly products with home delivery options for the 51+ group.\n",
        "\n",
        "4. City-Based Strategy\n",
        "\n",
        "City Category B customers spend the most; allocate more marketing budget and inventory to these locations.\n",
        "\n",
        "City Category C shows growth potential; offer regional and affordable deals to increase sales.\n",
        "\n",
        "Maintain premium product availability in City Category A to serve high-income shoppers.\n",
        "\n",
        "5. Product and Pricing Strategy\n",
        "\n",
        "Keep a balanced product range covering both budget and premium price segments (₹185–₹23,961).\n",
        "\n",
        "Use dynamic and data-driven pricing strategies during major sales events like Black Friday.\n",
        "\n",
        "Encourage cross-selling and add-on purchases to increase the average basket value.\n",
        "\n",
        "6. Marketing and Retention Strategy\n",
        "\n",
        "Use customer data to deliver personalized recommendations based on gender, age, and city.\n",
        "\n",
        "Increase digital ad targeting toward high-spending segments (male, age 26–50, City B).\n",
        "\n",
        "Strengthen customer loyalty programs and reward repeat shoppers through memberships and cashback offers.\n",
        "\n",
        "7. Strategic Summary\n",
        "\n",
        "Focus Area\tAction Item\tGoal\n",
        "Gender\tTarget males with premium products; increase female spending through offers\tBoost average transaction value\n",
        "Age\tFocus on 26–50-year-olds with relevant campaigns\tMaximize revenue\n",
        "City\tPrioritize City B and C stores\tIncrease market share\n",
        "Marital Status\tUse joint campaigns for both groups\tReduce marketing cost\n",
        "Product Range\tOffer both budget and premium products\tServe all income levels\n",
        "\n",
        "8. Overall Recommendation\n",
        "Walmart should strengthen data-driven marketing by targeting customers based on gender, age, and city.\n",
        "The 26–50 age group and male shoppers, especially in City Category B, are the most profitable segments.\n",
        "By focusing promotions, inventory, and pricing strategies on these key customer groups, Walmart can improve revenue, customer satisfaction, and overall sales performance."
      ],
